class:: TZSplitOsc
summary:: Through-zero oscillator with related spectral-section outputs
categories:: UGens>Generators>Deterministic
related:: Guides/NovelOscillators

description::

TZSplitOsc is a through-zero oscillator whose signed phase can reverse
continuously. code::symmetry:: introduces even harmonics, code::drive::
increases spectral density, and the even and odd sections can be processed
separately.

The asymmetric triangle corners use polynomial BLAMP correction. Drive runs in
an optionally oversampled domain and a fourth-order low-pass decimator returns
the result to the server sample rate. Reset and flip triggers are sample
accurate; if both arrive together, reset occurs first and flip second.

The closest vanilla UGen is link::Classes/LFTri::. Both provide triangle-like
motion, but TZSplitOsc supports signed through-zero frequency, BLAMP corners,
nonlinear drive, phase control, and a four-part output decomposition.

classmethods::

method:: ar

argument:: freq
Signed base frequency in hertz.

argument:: linearFM
Signed frequency offset in hertz. It may push the total frequency through zero.

argument:: phaseMod
Readout phase modulation in radians. It does not change accumulated phase.

argument:: symmetry
Triangle peak position, clamped to code::0.02..0.98::. code::0.5:: is
symmetric.

argument:: drive
Nonlinear shaping amount, clamped to code::1..16::. code::1:: is neutral.

argument:: reset
A nonpositive-to-positive edge resets phase to zero.

argument:: flip
A nonpositive-to-positive edge advances phase by half a cycle.

argument:: oversample
Initialization-rate value code::1::, code::2::, or code::4::.

argument:: mul
Multiplier applied to all four outputs.

argument:: add
Offset added to all four outputs after multiplication.

returns::
Four signals in this order: sine reference, even section, odd section, and
full shaped waveform. Even plus odd reconstructs full within floating-point
tolerance.

method:: kr

TZSplitOsc is audio-rate only. This method throws an error.

subsection:: Discussion

The even section becomes very small near code::symmetry = 0.5::. Oversampling
cost grows approximately with its factor and is most useful when drive or
rapid modulation produces strong upper partials.

examples::

code::
(
{ TZSplitOsc.ar(110, symmetry: 0.35, drive: 2)[3] * 0.08 }.play;
)
::

code::
(
{
    var signal = TZSplitOsc.ar(
        70,
        linearFM: SinOsc.ar(53, 0, 90),
        phaseMod: SinOsc.ar(2, 0, 2),
        symmetry: LFTri.kr(0.1).range(0.15, 0.85),
        drive: 3,
        oversample: 4
    );
    Splay.ar([signal[1], signal[2]]) * 0.055;
}.play;
)
::

